<GuideLayout slug="tree-removal-cost-south-florida">
  <p>
    "How much will it cost?" is the first question almost everyone asks about removing a tree. The honest answer is
    that two trees of the same height can cost very different amounts, because the price depends far more on
    <strong>where</strong> the tree is and <strong>how</strong> it has to come down than on the tree alone. This guide
    explains what drives the price, so an estimate makes sense when you get one.
  </p>

  <h2>The main factors</h2>

  <h3>1. Size of the tree</h3>
  <p>
    Height, trunk diameter and canopy spread decide how much wood there is to cut, lower and haul away. A small
    ornamental tree can be taken down in an hour; a mature live oak or banyan can take a full crew most of a day.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What's underneath and around it</h3>
  <p>
    A tree in an open yard can often be felled in larger sections. The same tree leaning over a roof, pool cage, fence
    or power line has to be taken down piece by piece, with each limb roped and lowered. That controlled work takes
    more time and more people, and it is the biggest single reason prices vary.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Access for equipment</h3>
  <p>
    If a truck, chipper or lift can reach the tree, the job goes faster. Backyard trees behind a house with a narrow side
    gate may need everything carried out by hand, or a crane to lift sections over the house.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Condition of the tree</h3>
  <p>
    Dead, rotten or storm-damaged trees can be unpredictable to climb and cut. They need extra care and sometimes
    different equipment, which can add to the cost even when the tree is smaller.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Crane work</h3>
  <p>
    For very large trees over structures, a crane is often the safest and fastest option. It adds the cost of the crane
    and operator, but it can shorten the job considerably and reduces the risk to your property.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Stump grinding and cleanup</h3>
  <p>
    Removing the tree and removing the stump are separate pieces of work. Ask whether stump grinding, root flare
    grinding and hauling away all wood and debris are included in the price you are quoted.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Permits and arborist reports</h3>
  <p>
    Some removals need a city permit, and some dangerous trees can be exempt with an arborist's written assessment. Both
    can add a fee and a little time. Our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a> explains when that applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Typical situations</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Situation</th><th>What usually affects the price</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Small tree in an open yard</td><td>Mostly time to cut and haul away; the simplest kind of job.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Palm tree near a pool or house</td><td>Height and how carefully it has to be lowered around pavers and screens.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large oak over the roof</td><td>Climbing, rigging each limb, and possibly a crane.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Storm-damaged or fallen tree</td><td>Tension in broken wood, damage already done, and urgency.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Several trees on one property</td><td>Usually cheaper per tree, since the crew and equipment are already there.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How to get an accurate estimate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Send photos of the whole tree and of what's around it: the house, fences, pool, power lines.</li>
    <li>Mention access: is there a gate, and how wide is it?</li>
    <li>Say whether you want the stump ground and the wood hauled away.</li>
    <li>If the tree is damaged after a storm, say so; that changes how it has to be handled.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Be careful with prices that seem too good to be true. A crew without proper insurance or equipment can turn a cheap
    removal into an expensive repair. Ask for proof of insurance before any work starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Get a free estimate</h2>
  <p>
    Every tree is different, so we price each job after seeing it. <a href="/contact-us/">Send us the details</a>, and for a
    fallen or dangerous tree, mark it as an emergency.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
